A missing writer stopped trustd, and with trustd stopped nothing can evaluate trust.

trustd died at startup, five times over, on one selector:

    cider: UNRECOGNIZED -[__NSCFDictionary writeToURL:error:]
    libc++abi: terminating with uncaught exception of type NSException
    Terminating app due to uncaught exception NSException, reason:
      -[__NSCFDictionary writeToURL:error:]: unrecognized selector sent to instance

The READER, dictionaryWithContentsOfURL:error:, was declared and implemented all along; only the
WRITER was missing, so anything that writes its own file and reads it back dies on a selector it
never had. NSArray had exactly the same hole, since arrayWithContentsOfURL:error: is declared there
too, and it is fixed here as well rather than waiting to be found the same way.

NOTE THE FORMAT. The atomically: methods write XML; these write BINARY, which is what Foundation
changed to when it introduced them in 10.13. Reading sniffs the format either way, so the choice
only shows up in the bytes on disk, and the bytes on disk are what fidelity means here.

WHY IT MATTERS FAR BEYOND a missing convenience method. Traced from the other end this rung: no
trustd means no trust evaluation, and Apple own ramdisk fallback in SecTrustEvaluateIfNecessary then
returns SUCCESS while leaving the result kSecTrustResultInvalid. The timestamp verifier reads that
as errSecTimestampNotTrusted, abandons a signature it has already judged GOOD, and everything above
flattens it to kCMSSignerInvalidSignature. MoneyMoney shows that to its user as a damaged file.

MEASURED AFTER: trustd stderr goes from 2,328 bytes of repeated NSException to ZERO, and launchctl
reports it as pid 9 rather than dead.

--- a/NSDictionary.m
+++ b/NSDictionary.m
@@ -986,6 +986,37 @@
     return success;
 }
 
+/*
+ * macOS 10.13. Note the FORMAT: the atomically: methods above write XML, this one writes BINARY,
+ * which is what Foundation changed to when it introduced these. A caller that writes with one and
+ * reads with the other still works, because property-list reading sniffs the format, so the choice
+ * only shows up in the bytes on disk -- but the bytes on disk are what fidelity means here.
+ */
+- (BOOL)writeToURL:(NSURL *)url error:(NSError **)error
+{
+    CFErrorRef cfError = NULL;
+    NSData *data = (NSData *)CFPropertyListCreateData(kCFAllocatorDefault, (CFPropertyListRef)self,
+                                                     kCFPropertyListBinaryFormat_v1_0, 0, &cfError);
+
+    if (data == nil)
+    {
+        if (error != NULL)
+        {
+            *error = [(NSError *)cfError autorelease];
+        }
+        else if (cfError != NULL)
+        {
+            CFRelease(cfError);
+        }
+        return NO;
+    }
+
+    BOOL success = [data writeToURL:url options:NSDataWritingAtomic error:error];
+
+    [data release];
+    return success;
+}
+
